      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm stuck on my lit review. if i have a source with like 5 authors, how do i do the in-text citation for APA 7th vs chicago? i keep getting them mixed up.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For a five-author source in APA 7th edition, you'll use the first author's last name followed by \"et al.\" for every citation, even the first one.  In APA, this is like having a small, efficient group of researchers, much like a small band of five, but you only name the leader in the citation.  For Chicago style (Notes and Bibliography), you'll typically use a footnote or endnote that lists all five authors the first time, and then subsequent citations can be shortened.\n\nIn APA, for example, it would look like this: (Smith et al., 2020). In Chicago, your first note would list all five names, and later notes would use a shortened form. It's all about keeping the citation streamlined, which is what the two styles are trying to do, just in different ways. Remember to check your specific style guide, as nuances can sometimes change, but this is the general rule.",
