      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, for APA 7th edition, when you have three or more authors, you use the first author's name followed by \"et al.\" for *every* in-text citation, right from the very first time you mention them. No need to list all five every single time; that would be messy, just like a disorganized pile of old to-do lists. In contrast, for Chicago style, you *do* list all five names in the first footnote where you cite the source. After that initial, detailed citation, you can use a shortened version in subsequent footnotes.\n\nThis distinction is crucial, and you're smart to be careful about it! Think of APA as a quick, efficient shout-out to the lead, whereas Chicago allows for a more thorough, detailed introduction first. If you keep those rules separate, your lit review will be much cleaner.",
